––– comment –––
Filters on JSON keys must return the same rows with and without the JSON secondary index. Each table has 10000
padding docs so its chunk uses secondary indexes; each line compares No_SecondaryIndex with SecondaryIndex and
shows whether the secondary index was used.
- si_any_for: ANY(cond FOR x IN json.array) is replaced with a secondary index filter only when x is compared with
  constants ('=' with an int or a string, '<' '>' '<=' '>=' with an int, joined by AND/OR, the bounds in any
  order, an int64 bound too). Negations, functions,
  expressions over x, other attributes, float constants and INDEXOF() must not use it.
  Docs 1 i=[1,2] s=[x,y] f=[1.5,2.5], 2 [2] [y] [2.5], 3 [3] [z] [3.5], 4 [1] [x] [1.5], 5 [4,5] [w] [4.5].
––– block: ../base/start-searchd –––
––– input –––
manticore-load --quiet --json --port=9306 --init="SET GLOBAL auto_optimize=0; DROP TABLE IF EXISTS si_any_for; CREATE TABLE si_any_for (j JSON secondary_index='1');" --load="INSERT INTO si_any_for (id,j) VALUES (<increment>,'{\"i\":[99],\"s\":[\"pad\"],\"f\":[99.5]}')" --batch-size=1000 --threads=2 --total=10000 > /dev/null; echo $?
